// facet_device/permissions — may this application use the camera?
//
// The question every device capability gates on, asked once and in one
// vocabulary, so an application does not learn four answers to it.
//
// THIS FILE IS THE NEUTRAL BASE. `permissions_linux.cplus` is the sibling the
// resolver picks on Linux; a target with no sibling lands HERE, and the
// honesty matters more than the convenience — an app that asks on a platform
// with no backend is told `Unavailable` and `supported()` answers false,
// rather than being handed a `Granted` nobody checked. That is the shape
// `pty::supported` already uses, and it is why `supported` exists at all: a
// caller can ask instead of discovering it by being denied.
//
// ---- the four answers, and why there are four ---------------------------------
//
// `Granted` / `Denied` are the two everyone expects. The other two are what
// stop an application from lying to its user:
//
//   * `Undetermined` is NOT "denied". Nobody has been asked yet, and `request`
//     is what resolves it. An app that treats it as denial hides its own
//     feature behind a prompt it never showed; an app that treats it as a grant
//     opens a device it has no permission for. Both are common and both come
//     from a two-state enum.
//   * `Unavailable` is NOT "denied" either. There is no camera, or no
//     permission service, so no amount of asking will change the answer and
//     the UI should say so instead of offering a button that cannot work.
//
// ---- what this slice does not do ---------------------------------------------
//
// It answers the GATE. It does not open a camera, read a microphone or start a
// location stream — those are the capabilities themselves, and each is its own
// piece of work with its own platform story. A permission API is the part they
// all share, so it is the part that is worth having first.

enum Capability {
    Camera,
    Microphone,
    Location,
    Notifications,
}

enum Permission {
    /// The application may use it now.
    Granted,
    /// The user said no. Asking again is the user's decision to revisit, not
    /// the application's to force.
    Denied,
    /// Nobody has been asked. `request` is what resolves this.
    Undetermined,
    /// There is no such device, or no permission service to ask. Asking again
    /// will not help.
    Unavailable,
}

// Is there a permission system on this platform at all? False here, because
// this file is the platform WITHOUT one — a target that lands on the neutral
// base has no backend, not a permissive one.
fn supported() -> bool { return false; }

// Never prompts. This is the read an application makes while building a
// screen, and a read that could put a modal in front of the user is not a read.
fn status(c: Capability) -> Permission { return Permission::Unavailable; }

// May prompt, and blocks until the user has answered. `Undetermined` is the
// only status this can usefully change.
// `parent` is the platform's identifier for the window a consent dialog belongs
// to — the Linux half documents what it costs to omit it.
fn request(c: Capability, parent: str = "") -> Permission { return Permission::Unavailable; }

// The capability's name, for a diagnostic or a test. Not for display: an
// application shows its own words, in its own language.
fn name_of(c: Capability) -> str {
    match c {
        Capability::Camera => { return "camera"; }
        Capability::Microphone => { return "microphone"; }
        Capability::Location => { return "location"; }
        Capability::Notifications => { return "notifications"; }
    }
}
